Chronomelt
Ingredients: SKATE PUNK & POP PUNK + OLD SKOOL JUNGLE & DRUM 'N' BASS + COZY, WARM & CHILL LO-FI + ELECTRIC SITAR
Creative Catalyst: Its instruments are built from meteorite fragments and antique clockwork.

Imagine the raw, fast-paced energy of skate punk and pop punk guitars, delivered with a lo-fi warmth and slight distortion, suddenly colliding head-on with the frenetic, intricate breakbeats and deep sub-bass of old skool jungle and drum 'n' bass. Overlaid on this high-octane rhythmic and melodic clash, an electric sitar weaves shimmering, psychedelic melodies and drones, sometimes sweet and hazy, sometimes distorted and aggressive, all bound together by a cozy, almost nostalgic sonic blanket of tape hiss and vinyl crackle.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: Energetic and relentless, typically ranging from 160-180 BPM, driven by complex, syncopated breakbeats (often sampled and re-pitched) fused with a punk rock backbeat and driving basslines.
*   Harmonic Palette: Leans into the catchy, often major-key melodicism of pop punk, but frequently introduces modal scales and subtle microtonal inflections from the electric sitar, creating a bittersweet, almost melancholic-psychedelic undertone within the otherwise upbeat framework.
*   Production Signatures:
    1.  "Meteorite Grain" Breaks: Heavily compressed, often crunchy drum breaks processed with noticeable tape saturation and subtle, almost metallic-sounding reverb, hinting at the raw materials of their origin.
    2.  Clockwork Melodies: Electric sitar melodies and drones are frequently run through vintage phasers, flangers, and wah pedals, creating a distinctive shimmering, almost ticking resonance that intertwines with distorted punk guitar riffs.
    3.  Lo-Fi Warmth Filters: A pervasive use of low-pass filtering and high-pass filtering, coupled with vinyl crackle, tape hiss, and subtle analog hum, to give the entire mix a cozy, worn, and timeless quality.
*   Dynamics: Tracks typically start with a hazy, atmospheric sitar or guitar intro, quickly exploding into high-energy punk-jungle verses and choruses, often featuring sudden, dramatic drops into half-time, bass-heavy breakdowns before surging back into full-throttle chaos, always underpinned by a warm, pulsing lo-fi foundation.

Scene & Origin Lore
Chronomelt first bubbled up in the forgotten industrial zones of what was once "Clockwork Alley," a district known for its abandoned factories and a peculiar meteor shower decades prior, where a generation of restless kids found solace in skate parks and derelict spaces. They scavenged antique clockwork mechanisms and strange, iridescent meteorite fragments, repurposing them into rudimentary instruments and effects pedals. This DIY spirit, coupled with late-night pirate radio broadcasts of jungle and the raw energy of local punk shows, birthed Chronomelt. The fashion was a mix of distressed skatewear, custom patches made from circuit boards, and repurposed metallic adornments, all worn with a defiant, warm grit. Their rituals involved clandestine skate sessions that transitioned into basement jams, fueled by the unique sonic resonance of their meteor-sharded, clockwork-hearted gear.

Typical Instruments Used: Electric sitar, heavily distorted electric guitars (often played through vintage fuzz and wah), raw bass guitar, sampled breakbeats (Amen, Think, Funky Drummer) processed with heavy compression and tape saturation, analog synthesizers (for pads and sub-bass), and various DIY effects pedals incorporating antique clockwork gears and meteorite dust.

For Fans Of: Bad Brains, Roni Size, Boards of Canada, Cornershop

